      "problem": "$6{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}=$",
      "worked_solution": "To raise the fraction to the power of $3$, we must raise both the numerator and the denominator to the power of $3.$ So we have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}=\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{3}^{3}}=\\frac{8}{27}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n6{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}=6\\cdot \\frac{8}{27}=\\frac{48}{27}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{48}{27}=\\frac{16\\cdot 3}{9\\cdot 3}=\\frac{16}{9}\n$$",
